\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Finite spectral triples and the Connes distance}
\date{2026-11-08}

\p{The naive lattice Dirac operator does not reproduce lattice
spacing.}

\p{\citet{equation (45)}{bimonte1994lattice} computes the distances
along one axis. For one, two, three and 4 steps they are #{2a},
#{2\sqrt{2}\,a}, #{4a} and #{2\sqrt{6}\,a}. Those are not the
multiples #{a}, #{2a}, #{3a} and #{4a}.}

\p{A Wilson term multiplies every one of those distances by
#{(1+r^{2})^{-1/2}}.}

\p{\citet{the paragraph containing (47)}{bimonte1994lattice} records
that rescaling.}

\p{Let the three points be labelled #{1}, #{2} and #{3}, and let real
functions on those points carry the counting inner product #{h}.
Let #{D} be a self-adjoint endomorphism of that function space, and
let a real function #{f} act by multiplication. Write
#{D_{ij}=h(\delta_i,D\delta_j)}. The distance from point #{1} to
point #{2} is the supremum of #{|f(2)-f(1)|} over all #{f} with
#{|[D,f]|\le 1}. Set
##{\kappa=D_{12}^{2}D_{13}^{2}+D_{12}^{2}D_{23}^{2}+D_{13}^{2}D_{23}^{2}.}
If #{\kappa>0}, the distance is
##{\sqrt{\frac{D_{13}^{2}+D_{23}^{2}}{\kappa}}.}
If #{D_{13}=D_{23}=0} and #{D_{12}\ne 0}, the distance is
#{1/|D_{12}|}. In every remaining case there is no finite upper
bound. The distances among the other pairs are the same statements
with the indices cycled.}

\proof{
\p{Declare the counting basis of the three indicators. In that basis
the matrix entries of #{D} are #{D_{ij}}, and multiplication
by #{f} is the diagonal matrix with entries #{f_i=f(i)}. The commutator has entries #{[D,f]_{ij}=D_{ij}(f_j-f_i)}. Adding a
constant to #{f} does not change it, so set #{f_1=0}, #{f_2=x} and
#{f_3=y}. The entries above the diagonal are #{a=D_{12}x},
#{b=D_{13}y} and #{c=D_{23}(y-x)}. Let
#{\omega=(-c,b,-a)} and let #{s^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}}. Write
#{M} for the matrix of #{[D,f]}. It is skew-symmetric, so its square
is the negative of #{M^{\mathrm T}M}. Expanding that product gives
#{M^{\mathrm T}M=s^{2}\bbI -\omega\omega^{\mathrm T}}. Thus
#{M^{\mathrm T}M} fixes the line through #{\omega} by #{0} and acts
as #{s^{2}} on the orthogonal plane. The operator norm of
#{[D,f]} is therefore #{s}. The constraint #{s\le 1} reads
##{(D_{12}^{2}+D_{23}^{2})x^{2}-2D_{23}^{2}xy+(D_{13}^{2}+D_{23}^{2})y^{2}\le 1.}
}

\p{Write #{A=D_{13}^{2}+D_{23}^{2}}. If #{A>0}, the left side is a
quadratic polynomial in #{y} opening upwards. The discriminant of
this polynomial set equal to #{1} is #{4A-4\kappa x^{2}}. A real
#{y} exists exactly when this discriminant
is nonnegative. If #{\kappa>0}, that condition is
#{x^{2}\le A/\kappa}, so the largest value of #{|x|} is the displayed square
root. If #{\kappa=0}, the discriminant is positive for every #{x},
and there is no finite upper bound.}

\p{If #{A=0}, then #{D_{13}=D_{23}=0} and the constraint reduces to
#{D_{12}^{2}x^{2}\le 1}. A nonzero #{D_{12}} gives the reciprocal
bound. If #{D_{12}} vanishes as well, every #{x} is allowed. Since
#{A=0} forces #{\kappa=0}, this case and the case #{A>0} with
#{\kappa=0} cover every vanishing #{\kappa}.}

\p{On the path with vertices #{0,1,2,3}, declare the counting basis of
vertex indicators and let #{D} be the adjacency operator. Its matrix
in that basis is the adjacency matrix. The same commutator norm gives distance #{2} from #{0} to
#{3}. The graph distance along the three edges is #{3}. Let
#{a=f_1-f_0}, #{b=f_2-f_1} and #{c=f_3-f_2}, so
#{f_3-f_0=a+b+c}. The commutator is zero except for these three
adjacent pairs. Replacing each difference by its absolute value does
not change the operator norm: the signs are absorbed by changing the
signs of the last three basis vectors. Thus it is enough to take
#{a,b,c\ge 0}.}

\p{Suppose #{a+b+c>2}. If #{a\ge 2}, the first basis vector is sent to
a vector of length #{a}, so the norm exceeds #{1}. If #{a<2}, then
#{b+c>2-a}. The vector #{(0,1,0,-1)} has length #{\sqrt{2}} and is
sent to #{(a,0,-(b+c),0)}, whose square length is
#{a^{2}+(b+c)^{2}}. That square exceeds
#{a^{2}+(2-a)^{2}=2(a-1)^{2}+2\ge 2}, so the operator norm exceeds
#{1}. Therefore every admissible triple has #{a+b+c\le 2}.}

\p{The values #{a=c=1} and #{b=0} give #{f=(0,1,1,2)}. The commutator
is the orthogonal sum of two copies of the block with entries
#{0,1,-1,0}, and each block has norm #{1}. The difference of the
endpoint values is #{2}, so the distance is exactly #{2}.}
}

\p{For a real symmetric matrix on #{4} indices,
\citet{Proposition 4}{iochum1999distances} gives a finite distance
exactly when the two indices lie in one component of the graph of
its nonzero off-diagonal entries. A function constant on each
component commutes with the matrix, so a pair in different
components has unbounded separation. A finite distance in this
setting is a root of a polynomial of degree at most #{12}, and
that polynomial is generically not solvable by radicals, by
\citet{Theorem 9}{iochum1999distances}. The path above is one
matrix for which the distance can still be read off directly. The
degree count stays with the source.}
